course: chemistry
lesson: 4
title: Solids, liquids and gases
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Most things around us are solids, liquids or gases, and the difference is how their particles behave.
explain: In a solid like a wooden block the particles are locked in place and only wobble, so it keeps its shape.
explain: In a liquid like juice the particles stay close but slide past each other, so the juice takes the shape of a tall glass or a wide bowl but keeps the same amount.
explain: In a gas like the air in a balloon the particles fly about far apart, so a gas fills any space and can be squashed.
explain: Some things are tricky: toothpaste, ketchup and cornflour slime behave like a solid one moment and a liquid the next.
fact: A solid keeps its own shape because its particles are held in fixed places and only wobble.
fact: A liquid takes the shape of its container but keeps the same volume; it can hardly be squashed.
fact: A gas spreads out to fill all the space it is in, and it can be squashed because its particles are far apart.
fact: Squashed air pushes back hard, which is how bike tyres and footballs work.
fact: A blown-up balloon shrinks a little in the cold and grows again when warmed.
fact: Sand pours like a liquid, but each grain is a tiny solid.
fact: Toothpaste and ketchup are fine solid bits in a liquid; they sit still until pushed hard enough, then flow.
fact: Cornflour and water runs like a liquid when moved slowly but goes hard when punched quickly, because a quick push jams the grains together.
fact: Real steam is an invisible gas; the white cloud from a kettle is tiny water droplets formed as the steam cools, so the real steam is in the clear gap right at the spout.
fact: Steam can burn worse than boiling water, because it gives out extra heat as it turns back into water.
fact: Fizzy water has carbon dioxide gas dissolved in it under pressure; the gas escapes as bubbles, and warm drinks lose it faster; the bubbles rise because gas is much lighter than the liquid around them.
fact: A flame is glowing hot gas; its yellow light comes from tiny specks of hot soot.
fact: Lightning and glowing signs contain plasma, a fourth state: gas so hot or charged that its atoms lose electrons.
fact: Glass is a solid with jumbled particles; the idea that old windows are thick at the bottom because glass flows is a myth: old panes were simply made uneven, and some are thicker at the top.
exercise: You pour juice from a tall glass into a wide bowl. What changes? | Its shape changes but the amount stays the same | Its amount gets smaller in a wide bowl | It turns partly into a gas | Its shape and amount both stay the same
exercise: Why does a wooden block keep its shape? | Its particles are locked in place and only wobble | Its particles are far apart and fly around | Its particles slide past each other | It has no particles inside it
exercise: You block the end of a syringe and push the plunger. Why does it move in a bit? | The air particles are far apart and can be squashed closer | The air leaks out slowly through tiny holes in the plastic | The plunger shrinks when you push | The air turns into water
exercise: Why does the air in a balloon fill the whole balloon evenly? | Gas particles fly in every direction and fill any space | Air is pulled out by the stretchy rubber | The air only fills the top half | Your breath is pushed in by a pump
exercise: Toothpaste sits in a heap but flows out when you squeeze. Why? | It is solid bits in a liquid that flows only when pushed hard | It melts from the warmth of your hand | It is a gas squashed inside the tube | It is a pure solid that breaks into pieces
exercise: Cornflour slime feels hard when you punch it. Why? | A quick push jams the grains together | The punch freezes the water in the slime | The cornflour turns into plastic for a moment | Your fist heats it up into a hard solid
exercise: Where is the real steam at a kettle spout? | In the clear gap right at the spout | In the white cloud further out | Inside the kettle lid only | Nowhere, steam is only white smoke
exercise: What makes the bubbles in fizzy water? | Carbon dioxide gas escaping from the liquid | Air sucked in from the top of the glass by the cold drink | Tiny drops of oil in the water | Water boiling inside the glass
exercise: What is a candle flame mostly? | Glowing hot gas with specks of hot soot | A solid that is made of light | A liquid made of melted wax, floating above the wick | Pure smoke with no gas in it
exercise: Is glass a slow liquid that drips down old windows? | No, it is a solid that does not flow at room temperature | Yes, it drips about a centimetre a year, so old windows get thick at the bottom | Yes, but only in warm countries | No, glass is a gas trapped in a shape
exercise: What is plasma, the fourth state in lightning? | A gas so hot or charged that its atoms lose electrons | A very thick and sticky liquid, like honey or treacle | A very cold solid made of ice | A mixture of smoke and water
exercise: Why can you hardly squash a closed, full water bottle? | Liquid particles are already close together | Water is really a solid in disguise | The plastic is much too strong to bend | Water particles are very far apart
exercise: Is sand a solid or a liquid? | A solid, made of many tiny grains | A liquid, because it pours like water | A gas, because it blows in the wind | Neither, it is a special kind of plasma
exercise: Why does ketchup sit in the bottle and then come out all at once? | It flows only once it is pushed or shaken hard enough | The bottle sucks it back in until the air inside has warmed up enough | Ketchup is a gas until it touches air | It melts suddenly in the bottle
test: Your friend says: "Air is nothing, you can't push against it." What does the syringe show? | Air is a gas that pushes back when squashed | Air really is nothing at all | Air is a liquid that cannot be squashed at all | Air only pushes back when it is wet
test: What would happen to a blown-up balloon left in a cold car overnight? | It would shrink a little and grow back when warmed | It would burst from the cold | It would grow bigger and bigger in the cold until it popped | It would turn into a liquid
test: "steam's just hot water innit, it can't burn worse" Is that right? | No, steam gives out extra heat as it turns back to water | Yes, steam is always much cooler than the boiling water it came from | Yes, steam can't burn at all | No, steam burns because it is on fire
test: The tour guide says the old church windows are thick at the bottom because glass flows. What do you say? | It is a myth; old panes were made uneven and some are thicker at the top | He is right, glass is a liquid that has been slowly dripping down for hundreds of years | He is right, but only in cold churches | Glass only flows under water
test: In a cartoon, someone pours a gas into a jar and it sits at the bottom like water, filling half. What is wrong? | Air-like gases spread to fill the whole jar | Nothing, gases always fill exactly half of whatever they are poured into | Gases always float out of jars | Gases turn solid in jars
test: Your sister asks: "Is fire a solid, a liquid or a gas?" Best answer? | Mostly glowing hot gas | A solid you can't touch | A liquid made of pure light | None, fire is not made of anything
test: Why do bubbles in a glass of fizzy drink always rise? | Gas is much lighter than the liquid around it | Bubbles are pulled up by the light shining down on the glass | The glass pushes them up from the sides | Bubbles are filled with helium
test: "why can't I squash water in the syringe but I can squash air, makes no sense" Best answer? | Water's particles are already close, air's are far apart | Water is heavier than the syringe, so the plunger cannot lift it | Air is slippery and water is sticky | It is only because the plunger is wet
test: Your mate says slime is just a liquid. What happens if he punches a big bowl of cornflour slime? | It goes hard under his fist | His fist splashes right through | It turns into a cloud of gas | It freezes solid and stays solid
test: What would happen to fizzy water left warm and open on a table? | It would lose its gas faster and go flat | It would get more and more fizzy as it warms up | It would slowly dry out into a sticky solid | Nothing at all would change while it sits there
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the behaviour of a liquid? | It assumes the shape of its container whilst retaining a fixed volume | It retains its own shape irrespective of any container into which it is placed | It expands so as to occupy the entirety of any container | It may be compressed into a fraction of its volume
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a gas, unlike a liquid, may be readily compressed. | The particles of a gas are widely separated from one another | The particles of a gas are considerably larger than those of a liquid | Gases are devoid of particles altogether | The particles of a gas are held in fixed positions
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the visible white cloud issuing from a kettle. | It consists of minute water droplets condensed from invisible steam | It consists of steam, which is invariably white and visible at all temperatures | It consists of smoke produced by the heating element | It consists of air rendered visible by heat
exam: It is asserted that window glass is a liquid flowing imperceptibly. Determine the validity of this assertion. | The assertion is a myth, glass being a solid at ordinary temperatures | The assertion is correct in all respects | The assertion is correct for coloured glass alone | The assertion is correct solely in warm climates
